  "verbalization": "Levallorphan, Pentazocine, and Dezocine share a common structural similarity, which is reflected in their classification as organonitrogen compounds and their ability to bind to opioid receptors. This structural overlap and functional similarity suggest that Levallorphan may act through a mechanism similar to that of Pentazocine and Dezocine, which are known to activate opioid receptors. The binding of Dezocine to Oprm1, a key opioid receptor, indicates that Levallorphan may also exert its effects through this pathway. The evidence from multiple paths highlights a recurring mechanism of structural resemblance and receptor binding, reinforcing the hypothesis that Levallorphan interacts with Oprm1 via an opioid receptor agonist mechanism. The shared classification as organonitrogen compounds and the presence of a lowest common ancestor in the pathway further support this relationship, suggesting a unified pharmacological profile among these compounds.",
